     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order yet. At 1700 mg three times daily, the total is 5100 mg/day, which is about twice the maximum labeled dose of metformin (2550 mg/day for immediate-release, 2000 mg/day for extended-release). Doses this high raise the risk of lactic acidosis, and 1700 mg isn't a standard tablet strength (common strengths are 500, 850, and 1000 mg).\n\nPlease confirm the intended dose. For example:\n- **850 mg PO TID with meals** (2550 mg/day, the labeled IR maximum)\n- **1000 mg PO BID with meals** (2000 mg/day)\n- **500 mg PO TID with meals** (1500 mg/day)\n- Or **1700 mg** as written, if you intend it and want it entered as is.\n\nOnce you confirm, I'll enter the order. Since the patient is 70 years old, you may also want to check renal function (eGFR) before starting, though you've noted it's normal."
      }
     ],
